  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "biomechanics-collective-fb88",
  "summary": "Recent preprints pair analytical reduction and agent-based simulation to explain fate, fixation, and granuloma progression in structured cell populations.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these three preprints is mathematical and computational modeling of multicellular populations, where spatial organization or fixation emerges from reduced equations or agent-based rules. A two-phenotype go-or-grow tumor model under cyclic hypoxia is reduced to a single oxygen-dependent equation and validated by numerical simulations. A CompuCell3D agent-based model of tuberculosis granuloma progression is compared with a prior within-host model and tested for parameter-dependent behavior. Agent-based simulations and a drift-diffusion model show that cell morphology and division rate govern fixation in dual bacterial populations. What is new is the pairing of analytical reduction with simulation-based validation. Because the systems differ mechanistically, the shared element is methodological rather than mechanistic.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-03-03"
}
